{
  "case_id": "2007-06-937",
  "document_hash": "28e60cb28ae3f0b0e09fd298aef50551a722d009f24bff64e15283b5566bb108",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2007-06-937",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "28e60cb28ae3f0b0e09fd298aef50551a722d009f24bff64e15283b5566bb108",
      "evidence_id": "ev-28e60cb28ae3-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. MAHONEY: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court:\nUnder this Court's exhaustion cases,\nexhaustion has always been triggered when two criteria\nhave been satisfied and the district court properly\ndismissed these claims because it found that they were\nsatisfied here on the undisputed facts. The first is\nthat there must be an authorized sale under the patent\nthat was allegedly infringed. That's never been in\ndispute in this case. The Federal Circuit recognized\nthat Intel was authorized to sell these components under\nthe system and method patents at issue in the case that\nhave been allegedly infringed."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2007-06-937",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "28e60cb28ae3f0b0e09fd298aef50551a722d009f24bff64e15283b5566bb108",
      "evidence_id": "ev-28e60cb28ae3-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: Ms. Mahoney, can I just\nget one thing straight in my mind. Which transaction\ntriggered the exhaustion doctrine in your judgment, the\ngeneral license to Intel or the sale by Intel to Quanta."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States",
      "case_id": "2007-06-937",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "28e60cb28ae3f0b0e09fd298aef50551a722d009f24bff64e15283b5566bb108",
      "evidence_id": "ev-28e60cb28ae3-0000-0001",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 17,
        "start": 17
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. HUNGAR: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice,\nand may it please the Court:\nFor 150 years this Court has held that an\nauthorized sale removes the particular item sold from\nthe protection of the patent laws. The court below\nerroneously transformed that patent-exhaustion doctrine\nfrom a definitional principle that delimits the scope of\nthe patent grant into an optional default assumption\nthat can be discarded at the whim of the patentee."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2007-06-937",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "28e60cb28ae3f0b0e09fd298aef50551a722d009f24bff64e15283b5566bb108",
      "evidence_id": "ev-28e60cb28ae3-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And we submit that, although the Respondent essentially ignores or runs away from the rationale of the court of appeals, we submit it's important for this Court to explicitly address and explicitly reject the Federal Circuit's misunderstanding of the patent-exhaustion doctrine, its view that a patentee can essentially override it simply by attaching a notice to the article that has been sold in an authorized sale."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2007-06-937",
      "confidence": 0.96,
      "document_hash": "28e60cb28ae3f0b0e09fd298aef50551a722d009f24bff64e15283b5566bb108",
      "evidence_id": "ev-28e60cb28ae3-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Although you think it can be overridden simply by providing in the contract that the same rights and remedies would be available?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Hungar",
      "case_id": "2007-06-937",
      "confidence": 0.94,
      "document_hash": "28e60cb28ae3f0b0e09fd298aef50551a722d009f24bff64e15283b5566bb108",
      "evidence_id": "ev-28e60cb28ae3-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. HUNGAR: No, Your Honor. I mean, it depends a little bit on what contract we're talking about and what it says. It is true, as Justice Stevens indicated, it has always been true, that this Court has deemed a license under a patent to be different from a sale of a particular article under a patent. It is the sale of the article that exhausts. The license does not -- exhaustion doesn't -- isn't relevant at the mere licensing stage."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2007-06-937",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "28e60cb28ae3f0b0e09fd298aef50551a722d009f24bff64e15283b5566bb108",
      "evidence_id": "ev-28e60cb28ae3-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: The big key is what is an authorized sale? And I'm asking you if the -- if the license agreement to the -- to Intel had said you may only sell to people who agree to use the products on the patentee's products, that then would -- and they did otherwise, they didn't get -- then it would not have been an authorized sale?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2007-06-937",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "28e60cb28ae3f0b0e09fd298aef50551a722d009f24bff64e15283b5566bb108",
      "evidence_id": "ev-28e60cb28ae3-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOUTER: What --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2007-06-937",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "28e60cb28ae3f0b0e09fd298aef50551a722d009f24bff64e15283b5566bb108",
      "evidence_id": "ev-28e60cb28ae3-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: Then why isn't it in your case?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Intel",
      "case_id": "2007-06-937",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "28e60cb28ae3f0b0e09fd298aef50551a722d009f24bff64e15283b5566bb108",
      "evidence_id": "ev-28e60cb28ae3-0002-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. PHILLIPS: Well, Justice Breyer, we can quarrel about sort of the nature of the notice and what notice is adequate to do that, but the basic point here, which I think is indisputable, is that, one, the notice here is quite clear. It's one page. It's very specific. These are very sophisticated parties and they understood that they were not obtaining an implied license by purchasing the chips rather than going out and purchasing the systems."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2007-06-937",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "28e60cb28ae3f0b0e09fd298aef50551a722d009f24bff64e15283b5566bb108",
      "evidence_id": "ev-28e60cb28ae3-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: Is the reason that there's no implied license here, one, because you got the notice, or two, because the component has uses in other kinds of methods than the patented method?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2007-06-937",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "28e60cb28ae3f0b0e09fd298aef50551a722d009f24bff64e15283b5566bb108",
      "evidence_id": "ev-28e60cb28ae3-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: But it's subsequent conduct. If the implied license occurred, it didn't occur at the time of the sale; and it couldn't be -- it couldn't be negated at the time of the sale."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Intel",
      "case_id": "2007-06-937",
      "confidence": 0.8,
      "document_hash": "28e60cb28ae3f0b0e09fd298aef50551a722d009f24bff64e15283b5566bb108",
      "evidence_id": "ev-28e60cb28ae3-0002-0006",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 51,
        "start": 51
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "uncertain",
      "text": "MR. PHILLIPS: I think the answer to that is it shouldn't, that the exhaustion doctrine should be retained as a first-sale doctrine alone."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2007-06-937",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "28e60cb28ae3f0b0e09fd298aef50551a722d009f24bff64e15283b5566bb108",
      "evidence_id": "ev-28e60cb28ae3-0002-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. MAHONEY: I'd like to start by emphasizing what counsel did not say. He never said that Intel lacked the authority under the system and method patents to sell these components. He never said that. In fact, he said that Intel was released. Why were they released? This would have been contributory infringement, otherwise."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Univis",
      "case_id": "2007-06-937",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "28e60cb28ae3f0b0e09fd298aef50551a722d009f24bff64e15283b5566bb108",
      "evidence_id": "ev-28e60cb28ae3-0003-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Univis is on all fours. They say, well, that just involved a single patent. Well, as far as this case is concerned, it just involves a single patent, too."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "District Court",
      "case_id": "2007-06-937",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "28e60cb28ae3f0b0e09fd298aef50551a722d009f24bff64e15283b5566bb108",
      "evidence_id": "ev-28e60cb28ae3-0003-0001",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And the district court found that they would, and that's not in dispute."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Intel",
      "case_id": "2007-06-937",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "28e60cb28ae3f0b0e09fd298aef50551a722d009f24bff64e15283b5566bb108",
      "evidence_id": "ev-28e60cb28ae3-0003-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "For this, when Intel manufactured these chips, the microprocessors and the chipsets, it manufactured them in a way that embodied many of the"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "District Court",
      "case_id": "2007-06-937",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "28e60cb28ae3f0b0e09fd298aef50551a722d009f24bff64e15283b5566bb108",
      "evidence_id": "ev-28e60cb28ae3-0003-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "When the district court found no \"implied license,\" all the court was saying was, well, under the Federal Circuit precedent \"implied license\" is an \"equitable doctrine.\""
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2007-06-937",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "28e60cb28ae3f0b0e09fd298aef50551a722d009f24bff64e15283b5566bb108",
      "evidence_id": "ev-28e60cb28ae3-0003-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Thank you, Ms. Mahoney. The case is submitted."
    },
    {
